1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,623
Sweetheart, at my grandson's wedding,

2
00:00:02,623 --> 00:00:05,318
at a fancy country club in Cincinnati,

3
00:00:05,318 --> 00:00:07,800
the bride's mother handed me a tray

4
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:10,495
of dirty champagne glasses and told me

5
00:00:10,495 --> 00:00:12,480
to take them to the kitchen.

6
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:14,995
She thought I was the help,

7
00:00:14,995 --> 00:00:17,763
because of my plain dress and my

8
00:00:17,763 --> 00:00:18,628
old shoes.

9
00:00:18,808 --> 00:00:20,701
When I told her I was the

10
00:00:20,701 --> 00:00:23,882
groom's grandmother, she laughed and said,

11
00:00:23,882 --> 00:00:25,472
oh, that explains it.

12
00:00:25,652 --> 00:00:27,699
What she didn't know was who had

13
00:00:27,699 --> 00:00:29,170
raised her own husband.

14
00:00:29,519 --> 00:00:31,755
Tonight I'm going to tell you what

15
00:00:31,755 --> 00:00:33,991
that woman said to me all evening,

16
00:00:33,991 --> 00:00:36,555
what happened when her husband stood up

17
00:00:36,555 --> 00:00:37,739
to give his toast,

18
00:00:37,739 --> 00:00:39,646
and the one word he said that

19
00:00:39,646 --> 00:00:42,144
made two hundred people turn around in

20
00:00:42,144 --> 00:00:43,986
their chairs and look at me.

21
00:00:44,166 --> 00:00:45,140
Stay with me,

22
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:48,137
honey, because I've never seen a woman's

23
00:00:48,137 --> 00:00:50,685
face change color so many times in

24
00:00:50,685 --> 00:00:51,584
one evening.

25
00:00:51,935 --> 00:00:54,471
My grandson Jake is a good boy,

26
00:00:54,471 --> 00:00:55,371
sweetheart.

27
00:00:55,551 --> 00:00:57,919
A schoolteacher, like his mother.

28
00:00:58,099 --> 00:01:00,793
And in the summer of twenty nineteen,

29
00:01:00,793 --> 00:01:03,704
he married a lovely girl named Caroline,

30
00:01:03,704 --> 00:01:06,034
whose family had a great deal of

31
00:01:06,034 --> 00:01:06,470
money.

32
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:08,855
Her father owned a chain of car

33
00:01:08,855 --> 00:01:11,487
dealerships across Ohio and Kentucky,

34
00:01:11,487 --> 00:01:12,553
and her mother,

35
00:01:12,553 --> 00:01:15,327
Victoria, had planned the wedding for a

36
00:01:15,327 --> 00:01:15,683
year.

37
00:01:15,863 --> 00:01:18,754
It was at the Riverside Country Club.

38
00:01:18,934 --> 00:01:20,687
Four hundred dollars a plate.

39
00:01:21,037 --> 00:01:23,101
I wore my good navy dress from

40
00:01:23,101 --> 00:01:25,716
Penney's, the one I'd worn to Harold's

41
00:01:25,716 --> 00:01:28,606
funeral and my granddaughter's graduation.

42
00:01:28,786 --> 00:01:31,377
My pearls were the ones Harold gave

43
00:01:31,377 --> 00:01:34,042
me for our twenty-fifth anniversary,

44
00:01:34,042 --> 00:01:35,449
and they were fake,

45
00:01:35,449 --> 00:01:37,447
honey, and we both knew it.

46
00:01:37,627 --> 00:01:39,602
My shoes were black flats,

47
00:01:39,602 --> 00:01:42,945
because my knees are seventy-five years old.

48
00:01:43,125 --> 00:01:45,412
I thought I looked very nice.

49
00:01:45,592 --> 00:01:47,809
Victoria didn't agree.

50
00:01:48,159 --> 00:01:50,807
I'd never met Caroline's father.

51
00:01:50,987 --> 00:01:52,928
He'd been traveling all year,

52
00:01:52,928 --> 00:01:54,869
and he only flew in the night

53
00:01:54,869 --> 00:01:56,141
before the wedding.

54
00:01:56,321 --> 00:01:58,368
But I kept hearing his name,

55
00:01:58,368 --> 00:02:01,803
Richard Coleman, and something about it tickled

56
00:02:01,803 --> 00:02:03,264
the back of my mind.

57
00:02:03,444 --> 00:02:06,184
Like a song you can't quite remember.

58
00:02:06,364 --> 00:02:08,542
In a minute I'll tell you where

59
00:02:08,542 --> 00:02:10,439
I'd heard that name before.

60
00:02:10,619 --> 00:02:13,202
It took me forty-five years to put

61
00:02:13,202 --> 00:02:14,114
it together.

62
00:02:14,463 --> 00:02:15,980
At the cocktail hour,

63
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:18,146
I was standing by a table with

64
00:02:18,146 --> 00:02:19,374
a glass of punch,

65
00:02:19,374 --> 00:02:21,829
and Victoria came sweeping by in a

66
00:02:21,829 --> 00:02:24,284
silver gown, and she shoved a tray

67
00:02:24,284 --> 00:02:26,956
of empty glasses right into my hands.

68
00:02:27,136 --> 00:02:29,748
She said, these need to go back.

69
00:02:29,928 --> 00:02:32,227
And the shrimp is running low.

70
00:02:32,407 --> 00:02:33,684
Tell the kitchen.

71
00:02:33,864 --> 00:02:36,224
And she walked off before I could

72
00:02:36,224 --> 00:02:37,010
say a word.

73
00:02:37,190 --> 00:02:39,494
I stood there holding that tray like

74
00:02:39,494 --> 00:02:39,942
a fool.

75
00:02:40,291 --> 00:02:42,874
I found her a few minutes later

76
00:02:42,874 --> 00:02:44,791
and gave the tray back,

77
00:02:44,791 --> 00:02:45,957
very politely.

78
00:02:46,137 --> 00:02:48,623
I said, I think there's been a

79
00:02:48,623 --> 00:02:49,783
mistake, dear.

80
00:02:49,963 --> 00:02:51,089
I'm Ruth.

81
00:02:51,269 --> 00:02:52,859
Jake's grandmother.

82
00:02:53,039 --> 00:02:55,276
And she looked me up and down,

83
00:02:55,276 --> 00:02:57,588
honey, from my flat shoes to my

84
00:02:57,588 --> 00:02:59,750
fake pearls, and she laughed.

85
00:02:59,930 --> 00:03:01,660
A little tinkly laugh.

86
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:03,170
And she said,

87
00:03:03,170 --> 00:03:03,477
oh.

88
00:03:03,657 --> 00:03:05,108
Oh, of course.

89
00:03:05,288 --> 00:03:06,774
That explains it.

90
00:03:06,954 --> 00:03:09,292
And she turned to her friend and

91
00:03:09,292 --> 00:03:10,460
rolled her eyes.

92
00:03:10,810 --> 00:03:13,594
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

93
00:03:13,594 --> 00:03:15,741
tap the like button for me,

94
00:03:15,741 --> 00:03:16,615
sweetheart.

95
00:03:16,795 --> 00:03:20,081
It helps more people find these stories.

96
00:03:20,261 --> 00:03:22,200
It didn't stop there.

97
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:25,062
At dinner, I found my place card,

98
00:03:25,062 --> 00:03:28,069
and I was seated at table twenty-two.

99
00:03:28,249 --> 00:03:29,247
In the back.

100
00:03:29,427 --> 00:03:30,728
By the kitchen door.

101
00:03:30,908 --> 00:03:34,192
Next to the photographer's assistant and a

102
00:03:34,192 --> 00:03:36,225
cousin nobody had invited.

103
00:03:36,405 --> 00:03:38,739
Jake's parents were at table two.

104
00:03:38,919 --> 00:03:40,765
I was by the swinging door.

105
00:03:41,114 --> 00:03:42,396
And all evening,

106
00:03:42,396 --> 00:03:44,718
Victoria made little remarks,

107
00:03:44,718 --> 00:03:47,200
loud enough for me to hear when

108
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:48,080
she passed.

109
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:50,653
Some families just don't know how to

110
00:03:50,653 --> 00:03:52,115
dress for an occasion.

111
00:03:52,295 --> 00:03:55,189
And, I suppose we should be grateful

112
00:03:55,189 --> 00:03:56,556
they came at all.

113
00:03:56,736 --> 00:03:58,942
And once, to a waiter right next

114
00:03:58,942 --> 00:04:00,044
to me, she said,

115
00:04:00,044 --> 00:04:02,870
keep the wine away from table twenty-two,

116
00:04:02,870 --> 00:04:05,490
they won't know the difference anyway.

117
00:04:05,670 --> 00:04:07,393
I just smiled,

118
00:04:07,393 --> 00:04:08,131
honey.

119
00:04:08,311 --> 00:04:09,890
I'm good at smiling.

120
00:04:10,239 --> 00:04:12,829
My grandson came back to find me

121
00:04:12,829 --> 00:04:15,337
once, honey, and he saw where I

122
00:04:15,337 --> 00:04:18,169
was sitting, and his face went red.

123
00:04:18,349 --> 00:04:20,081
He said, Grandma,

124
00:04:20,081 --> 00:04:21,507
this is wrong.

125
00:04:21,687 --> 00:04:23,823
I'm going to say something.

126
00:04:24,003 --> 00:04:26,425
And I took his hand and said,

127
00:04:26,425 --> 00:04:28,763
Jake, it's your wedding day.

128
00:04:28,943 --> 00:04:31,514
Don't you dare spend one minute of

129
00:04:31,514 --> 00:04:32,194
it on me.

130
00:04:32,374 --> 00:04:34,150
Go dance with your bride.

131
00:04:34,330 --> 00:04:36,002
I'm fine right here.

132
00:04:36,182 --> 00:04:37,398
And I was,

133
00:04:37,398 --> 00:04:38,249
mostly.

134
00:04:38,429 --> 00:04:40,855
I'd had worse seats in my life.

135
00:04:41,205 --> 00:04:44,815
The photographer's assistant at my table was

136
00:04:44,815 --> 00:04:46,865
a sweet girl named Maria,

137
00:04:46,865 --> 00:04:50,146
about twenty, and she'd heard every word

138
00:04:50,146 --> 00:04:51,295
Victoria said.

139
00:04:51,475 --> 00:04:53,297
She leaned over and said,

140
00:04:53,297 --> 00:04:56,943
ma'am, my grandmother cleans houses in Cincinnati.

141
00:04:57,123 --> 00:04:59,415
People talk to her like that all

142
00:04:59,415 --> 00:05:00,060
the time.

143
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:01,194
And I said,

144
00:05:01,194 --> 00:05:04,663
honey, then your grandmother and I would

145
00:05:04,663 --> 00:05:05,964
get along fine.

146
00:05:06,144 --> 00:05:08,718
We spent the whole dinner talking about

147
00:05:08,718 --> 00:05:09,905
our grandchildren.

148
00:05:10,085 --> 00:05:11,839
Best company in the room.

149
00:05:12,188 --> 00:05:13,683
So far, the tray,

150
00:05:13,683 --> 00:05:16,496
the laugh, and table twenty-two.

151
00:05:16,676 --> 00:05:18,858
Then it was time for the toasts.

152
00:05:19,038 --> 00:05:20,938
The best man went first,

153
00:05:20,938 --> 00:05:23,946
and then Caroline's father stood up at

154
00:05:23,946 --> 00:05:25,134
the head table.

155
00:05:25,314 --> 00:05:28,585
A tall, silver-haired man in a beautiful

156
00:05:28,585 --> 00:05:28,994
suit.

157
00:05:29,174 --> 00:05:30,509
Richard Coleman.

158
00:05:30,689 --> 00:05:32,816
He took the microphone and said,

159
00:05:32,816 --> 00:05:35,275
I want to thank everybody for coming.

160
00:05:35,455 --> 00:05:37,096
And then he stopped,

161
00:05:37,096 --> 00:05:39,721
and he looked out over the room,

162
00:05:39,721 --> 00:05:41,608
slowly, table by table.

163
00:05:41,958 --> 00:05:44,321
And his eyes stopped at the back

164
00:05:44,321 --> 00:05:45,207
of the room,

165
00:05:45,207 --> 00:05:45,650
honey.

166
00:05:45,830 --> 00:05:47,595
At table twenty-two.

167
00:05:47,775 --> 00:05:48,797
At me.

168
00:05:48,977 --> 00:05:51,743
He stood there with that microphone for

169
00:05:51,743 --> 00:05:52,594
a long time,

170
00:05:52,594 --> 00:05:54,296
and the room went quiet,

171
00:05:54,296 --> 00:05:54,863
waiting.

172
00:05:55,043 --> 00:05:56,245
Then he said,

173
00:05:56,245 --> 00:05:59,296
very softly, into the microphone,

174
00:05:59,296 --> 00:06:00,221
I'm sorry.

175
00:06:00,401 --> 00:06:03,048
Excuse me, one moment.

176
00:06:03,228 --> 00:06:05,473
And he put the microphone down on

177
00:06:05,473 --> 00:06:07,650
the table, and he walked off the

178
00:06:07,650 --> 00:06:08,058
stage.

179
00:06:08,407 --> 00:06:10,786
He walked all the way through that

180
00:06:10,786 --> 00:06:12,815
ballroom, between the tables,

181
00:06:12,815 --> 00:06:15,123
past the cake and the dance floor

182
00:06:15,123 --> 00:06:17,641
and two hundred people in their best

183
00:06:17,641 --> 00:06:19,460
clothes, to the very back.

184
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:21,324
To table twenty-two.

185
00:06:21,504 --> 00:06:23,538
And he stood in front of me,

186
00:06:23,538 --> 00:06:25,354
and he looked at my face,

187
00:06:25,354 --> 00:06:27,751
and his own face crumpled up like

188
00:06:27,751 --> 00:06:28,841
a little boy's.

189
00:06:29,021 --> 00:06:31,517
And then he said one word.

190
00:06:31,697 --> 00:06:33,741
He said, Mama Ruth?

191
00:06:34,090 --> 00:06:35,618
And then I knew him,

192
00:06:35,618 --> 00:06:36,459
sweetheart.

193
00:06:36,639 --> 00:06:39,402
Forty-five years fell away in a second.

194
00:06:39,582 --> 00:06:40,940
Ricky Coleman.

195
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:43,871
A skinny, freckled boy from the house

196
00:06:43,871 --> 00:06:45,878
behind ours on Maple Drive,

197
00:06:45,878 --> 00:06:49,000
whose father drank and whose mother worked

198
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,710
nights at the hospital.

199
00:06:50,890 --> 00:06:53,130
From the time he was seven until

200
00:06:53,130 --> 00:06:54,250
he was fourteen,

201
00:06:54,250 --> 00:06:56,770
Ricky ate supper at my kitchen table

202
00:06:56,770 --> 00:06:58,100
almost every night.

203
00:06:58,280 --> 00:07:01,124
My children called him their other brother.

204
00:07:01,474 --> 00:07:02,869
When he was cold,

205
00:07:02,869 --> 00:07:05,166
I gave him Mark's old coats.

206
00:07:05,346 --> 00:07:07,412
When he got a bad report card,

207
00:07:07,412 --> 00:07:09,409
I sat with him at the kitchen

208
00:07:09,409 --> 00:07:11,888
table and did fractions until ten at

209
00:07:11,888 --> 00:07:12,301
night.

210
00:07:12,481 --> 00:07:14,967
When his father came home drunk and

211
00:07:14,967 --> 00:07:17,666
loud, Ricky would climb the back fence

212
00:07:17,666 --> 00:07:19,868
in his pajamas and sleep on our

213
00:07:19,868 --> 00:07:20,294
couch.

214
00:07:20,474 --> 00:07:22,903
Harold taught him to throw a football

215
00:07:22,903 --> 00:07:24,085
and change a tire.

216
00:07:24,265 --> 00:07:26,262
And he called me Mama Ruth.

217
00:07:26,611 --> 00:07:28,185
When he was fourteen,

218
00:07:28,185 --> 00:07:31,033
his mother finally left his father and

219
00:07:31,033 --> 00:07:32,757
moved them to Kentucky,

220
00:07:32,757 --> 00:07:34,556
and I lost track of him,

221
00:07:34,556 --> 00:07:35,005
honey.

222
00:07:35,185 --> 00:07:36,567
I wrote twice.

223
00:07:36,747 --> 00:07:38,384
The letters came back.

224
00:07:38,564 --> 00:07:41,050
I never knew what became of Ricky

225
00:07:41,050 --> 00:07:41,652
Coleman.

226
00:07:41,832 --> 00:07:43,346
And now here he was,

227
00:07:43,346 --> 00:07:45,843
on his knees by table twenty-two,

228
00:07:45,843 --> 00:07:48,113
at his own daughter's wedding,

229
00:07:48,113 --> 00:07:50,837
holding my hands and crying in front

230
00:07:50,837 --> 00:07:52,502
of two hundred people.

231
00:07:52,851 --> 00:07:55,861
Ricky told me later what happened after

232
00:07:55,861 --> 00:07:56,555
Kentucky.

233
00:07:56,735 --> 00:07:58,976
His mother got sick when he was

234
00:07:58,976 --> 00:08:01,651
seventeen, and he quit school to sell

235
00:08:01,651 --> 00:08:04,036
cars, washing them in the lot for

236
00:08:04,036 --> 00:08:04,398
tips.

237
00:08:04,578 --> 00:08:05,883
He was good at it,

238
00:08:05,883 --> 00:08:07,116
because, he said,

239
00:08:07,116 --> 00:08:09,437
he'd learned from me how to talk

240
00:08:09,437 --> 00:08:11,613
to anybody like they mattered.

241
00:08:11,793 --> 00:08:15,090
By thirty, he owned his first dealership.

242
00:08:15,270 --> 00:08:17,894
By fifty, he had nineteen.

243
00:08:18,074 --> 00:08:20,687
And he never stopped looking for me.

244
00:08:21,036 --> 00:08:21,900
He stood up,

245
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:23,341
and he took my hand,

246
00:08:23,341 --> 00:08:25,357
and he walked me all the way

247
00:08:25,357 --> 00:08:28,093
back through that ballroom to the head

248
00:08:28,093 --> 00:08:28,525
table.

249
00:08:28,705 --> 00:08:30,133
Every head turned.

250
00:08:30,313 --> 00:08:33,370
Then he picked up the microphone again

251
00:08:33,370 --> 00:08:34,335
and he said,

252
00:08:34,335 --> 00:08:36,025
I'm sorry, everybody.

253
00:08:36,205 --> 00:08:38,148
I need to tell you who this

254
00:08:38,148 --> 00:08:38,364
is.

255
00:08:38,544 --> 00:08:41,249
And I saw Victoria's face at the

256
00:08:41,249 --> 00:08:42,770
head table, honey.

257
00:08:42,950 --> 00:08:45,377
It was white as the tablecloth.

258
00:08:45,726 --> 00:08:48,266
Richard said, when I was a boy,

259
00:08:48,266 --> 00:08:50,069
my father was a drunk,

260
00:08:50,069 --> 00:08:52,363
and our house was not a safe

261
00:08:52,363 --> 00:08:52,855
place.

262
00:08:53,035 --> 00:08:55,314
And there was a woman who lived

263
00:08:55,314 --> 00:08:57,814
behind us, who fed me supper every

264
00:08:57,814 --> 00:08:59,432
night for seven years.

265
00:08:59,612 --> 00:09:01,330
Who taught me fractions,

266
00:09:01,330 --> 00:09:03,550
and bought me my first suit for

267
00:09:03,550 --> 00:09:05,196
the eighth grade dance.

268
00:09:05,376 --> 00:09:06,952
Every good thing I am,

269
00:09:06,952 --> 00:09:09,173
I learned at her kitchen table.

270
00:09:09,353 --> 00:09:11,837
I've looked for her for thirty years.

271
00:09:12,017 --> 00:09:13,399
And she's here.

272
00:09:13,579 --> 00:09:16,098
She's my daughter's new grandmother.

273
00:09:16,448 --> 00:09:17,970
And then he said,

274
00:09:17,970 --> 00:09:21,370
Mama Ruth, I named my first dealership

275
00:09:21,370 --> 00:09:22,265
after you.

276
00:09:22,445 --> 00:09:25,510
It's the Ruth Avenue Ford in Lexington.

277
00:09:25,690 --> 00:09:27,966
I never knew if you'd see it.

278
00:09:28,146 --> 00:09:30,056
The whole room stood up,

279
00:09:30,056 --> 00:09:30,932
sweetheart.

280
00:09:31,112 --> 00:09:32,604
Two hundred people,

281
00:09:32,604 --> 00:09:33,703
on their feet,

282
00:09:33,703 --> 00:09:34,409
clapping.

283
00:09:34,589 --> 00:09:37,178
My grandson Jake was crying.

284
00:09:37,358 --> 00:09:39,413
Caroline was crying.

285
00:09:39,593 --> 00:09:42,310
And Victoria was sitting there with her

286
00:09:42,310 --> 00:09:45,236
mouth open, holding her champagne glass in

287
00:09:45,236 --> 00:09:45,723
midair.

288
00:09:46,073 --> 00:09:48,523
And then Richard turned to his wife.

289
00:09:48,703 --> 00:09:50,186
He said, Victoria,

290
00:09:50,186 --> 00:09:53,068
where was Mama Ruth seated tonight?

291
00:09:53,248 --> 00:09:55,147
And Victoria said,

292
00:09:55,147 --> 00:09:58,101
very quietly, I didn't know.

293
00:09:58,281 --> 00:09:59,487
And Richard said,

294
00:09:59,487 --> 00:10:02,182
table twenty-two, by the kitchen door?

295
00:10:02,362 --> 00:10:03,988
A waiter told me.

296
00:10:04,168 --> 00:10:06,190
And a guest at that table told

297
00:10:06,190 --> 00:10:08,347
me what you said about the wine.

298
00:10:08,527 --> 00:10:11,523
Victoria didn't say anything at all.

299
00:10:11,703 --> 00:10:14,221
I've never seen anyone wish so hard

300
00:10:14,221 --> 00:10:16,091
to disappear into a chair.

301
00:10:16,441 --> 00:10:19,266
Richard had a waiter bring another chair,

302
00:10:19,266 --> 00:10:20,988
and he put me at the head

303
00:10:20,988 --> 00:10:23,674
table, right between him and the bride.

304
00:10:23,854 --> 00:10:26,248
And here's the part I love,

305
00:10:26,248 --> 00:10:26,780
honey.

306
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:28,741
My new granddaughter,

307
00:10:28,741 --> 00:10:31,793
Caroline, leaned over and whispered,

308
00:10:31,793 --> 00:10:34,761
Grandma Ruth, I'm so sorry about my

309
00:10:34,761 --> 00:10:35,354
mother.

310
00:10:35,534 --> 00:10:37,972
And will you teach me the fractions?

311
00:10:38,152 --> 00:10:40,487
I laughed so hard I spilled my

312
00:10:40,487 --> 00:10:43,133
punch on Richard's beautiful suit.

313
00:10:43,313 --> 00:10:45,588
He didn't mind one bit.

314
00:10:45,938 --> 00:10:49,039
Victoria apologized to me the next morning

315
00:10:49,039 --> 00:10:50,072
at the brunch,

316
00:10:50,072 --> 00:10:50,884
sweetheart.

317
00:10:51,064 --> 00:10:52,643
Stiffly, but she did it.

318
00:10:52,823 --> 00:10:54,518
And I'll give her credit.

319
00:10:54,698 --> 00:10:56,372
Every Christmas since,

320
00:10:56,372 --> 00:10:58,807
a card comes in her handwriting,

321
00:10:58,807 --> 00:11:00,329
and the second year,

322
00:11:00,329 --> 00:11:02,687
there was a pair of real pearls

323
00:11:02,687 --> 00:11:03,220
inside.

324
00:11:03,400 --> 00:11:05,130
I wear them to church.

325
00:11:05,310 --> 00:11:07,469
But the best thing that came out

326
00:11:07,469 --> 00:11:09,222
of that wedding was Ricky.

327
00:11:09,402 --> 00:11:10,192
Richard.

328
00:11:10,372 --> 00:11:12,939
He comes to my house every Christmas

329
00:11:12,939 --> 00:11:15,079
Eve now, and he climbs over my

330
00:11:15,079 --> 00:11:15,863
back fence.

331
00:11:16,043 --> 00:11:17,286
On purpose.

332
00:11:17,635 --> 00:11:20,321
Here's what I want you to remember,

333
00:11:20,321 --> 00:11:20,782
honey.

334
00:11:20,962 --> 00:11:23,423
You never know who's sitting at table

335
00:11:23,423 --> 00:11:24,154
twenty-two.

336
00:11:24,334 --> 00:11:26,353
The plain old woman in the cheap

337
00:11:26,353 --> 00:11:28,435
dress might be the reason the man

338
00:11:28,435 --> 00:11:30,581
at the head table turned out good.

339
00:11:30,761 --> 00:11:32,974
Treat everybody at the party like they're

340
00:11:32,974 --> 00:11:34,000
the guest of honor.

341
00:11:34,180 --> 00:11:36,871
Because to somebody in that room,

342
00:11:36,871 --> 00:11:37,605
they are.

343
00:11:37,954 --> 00:11:39,801
Tell me in the comments,

344
00:11:39,801 --> 00:11:40,648
sweetheart.

345
00:11:40,828 --> 00:11:43,232
Was there a neighbor or a teacher

346
00:11:43,232 --> 00:11:44,106
who fed you,

347
00:11:44,106 --> 00:11:45,562
or helped raise you,

348
00:11:45,562 --> 00:11:47,747
when things were hard at home?

349
00:11:47,927 --> 00:11:49,994
I'd love to hear about them.

350
00:11:50,174 --> 00:11:52,579
There are more of my family's stories

351
00:11:52,579 --> 00:11:54,854
in the playlist right there on your

352
00:11:54,854 --> 00:11:56,869
screen, and I'll see you in the

353
00:11:56,869 --> 00:11:57,454
next one.

354
00:11:57,634 --> 00:11:59,178
Good night, honey.
